---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout title="Projects & Research — Rahul Jain">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1 class="page-title">Projects &amp; Research</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">Things I've built and studied outside of my day job — production side projects, and research on how humans and machines learn.</p>

    <section>
      <div class="subsection">
        <h2 class="subsection-title">Projects</h2>

        <div class="project-card">
          <div class="project-title"><span>Mizen</span><span class="project-date">Winter 2025</span></div>
          <p class="project-desc">
            A structured data-extraction pipeline for recipes, built to bring the same rigor I apply to
            enterprise data to a much more chaotic domain: cooking sites. It's a dual-layer pipeline —
            deterministic JSON-LD schema enforcement runs first, with routing logic that falls back to a
            Llama 3.3-70B generative pass only when structured extraction fails. That fallback design was
            the interesting part: JSON-LD gets you clean, auditable output whenever a site is well-formed,
            and the LLM only has to handle the genuinely messy long tail. It extracts and normalizes data
            from 1,000+ live web sources and is in production at <a href="https://mizen.recipes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mizen.recipes</a> with real users.
          </p>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag">RAG</span>
            <span class="tag">LLM</span>
            <span class="tag">JSON-LD</span>
            <span class="tag">Llama 3.3</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="project-card">
          <div class="project-title"><span>Growth-Ring Audio Signatures</span><span class="project-date">Ongoing</span></div>
          <p class="project-desc">
            A generative visualization pipeline that turns a musical performance into a unique visual
            "signature." I extract timbral features — brightness, loudness — with librosa and render them
            as a covariance structure of concentric growth-ring ellipses, so the shape of the rings reflects
            the actual dynamics of the performance rather than an arbitrary waveform. Grew out of being a
            working musician and wanting a way to visually distinguish one performance from another. I've
            been exploring it as a format for physical art pieces or keepsakes — something you could give
            someone from a recording of a specific show.
          </p>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag">Audio Analysis</span>
            <span class="tag">librosa</span>
            <span class="tag">Generative Art</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="project-card">
          <div class="project-title"><span>Medley Vox</span><span class="project-date">Fall 2023</span></div>
          <p class="project-desc">
            Multi-singer detection and speaker diarization: an RNN model identifying overlapping vocal
            identities in a mixed audio track using 512-dimensional SpeechBrain x-vector embeddings — an
            embedding-based entity-resolution problem more than a traditional classification one. Outperformed
            two established benchmarks on held-out test data.
          </p>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag">RNN</span>
            <span class="tag">Speaker Diarization</span>
            <span class="tag">SpeechBrain</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="subsection">
        <h2 class="subsection-title">Research: Senior Thesis &amp; Hexxed</h2>
        <p class="blurb">
          My research asks a simple question with a complicated answer: when a human and a
          reinforcement-learning agent both learn the same skill, do they learn it the same way? At the
          W.M. Keck Science Center, I benchmarked RL agent behavior against 10,000+ human players on a
          complex skill-learning task called Hexxed, and found three consistent differences:
        </p>
        <dl class="skills-grid" style="margin-top:16px;">
          <dt>Strategy space</dt>
          <dd>Humans artificially narrow the space of strategies they'll consider, to master core rules first. Agents explore exhaustively by brute force.</dd>
          <dt>Persistence</dt>
          <dd>Humans stay with suboptimal strategies far longer than an agent would, seemingly to test their own mental model. Agents update systematically without that bias.</dd>
          <dt>Learning curve</dt>
          <dd>Human trajectories show non-linear "leaps" consistent with sudden insight. Agent learning is incremental, driven by gradient descent.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="blurb" style="margin-top:16px;">
          I presented these findings at the International Symposium on the Mathematics of Neuroscience
          in Rhodes, Greece, and again at the Science of Consciousness Conference. My senior thesis,
          <em>Evaluating Reinforcement Learning Agents in Hexxed</em> (advised by Joseph Osborn), extended
          this into a broader argument: aligning an artificial agent's internal representations with human
          abstract concepts may be a precondition for human-like few-shot learning, not just a nice-to-have.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="subsection">
        <h2 class="subsection-title">Research: Allen Institute (Mihalas Lab)</h2>
        <div class="entry-head" style="margin-bottom:4px;">
          <div><span class="entry-role">Research Collaborator</span></div>
          <div class="entry-date">Jul 2026 – Present</div>
        </div>
        <p class="blurb">
          Continuing the same thread from Hexxed and my senior thesis, now on the mechanistic side: I'm
          contributing to a computational framework unifying biologically-inspired predictive neural
          networks with attention-based memory mechanisms, studying how networks encode and retrieve
          working memory over time. Specifically, I'm looking at how heterogeneous per-synapse learning
          rates — different parts of a network learning at different speeds, closer to how biological
          synapses work — change what a network retains and prioritizes from recent experience. The
          theoretical predictions have been validated across 16 complex sequential decision-making tasks,
          and a paper is in preparation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="subsection">
        <h2 class="subsection-title">On AI and Cognitive Agency</h2>
        <p class="blurb">
          All of this feeds into how I think about AI product design. If people learn through productive
          struggle — narrowing their own options, testing bad ideas, occasionally leaping to insight —
          then an AI system that removes all friction from a task doesn't just save time, it can
          short-circuit the process that builds real understanding. I'm interested in how AI "thought
          partners" can be designed to collaborate with users in a way that sharpens judgment rather than
          substituting for it, drawing on the Ash &amp; Wiley impasse-restructuring mechanism and recent
          work connecting productive struggle to durable learning (Collins et al., 2024, <em>Nature Human
          Behaviour</em>).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="subsection">
        <h2 class="subsection-title">Publications &amp; Presentations</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Jain, R. "You Got Hexxed: Persistence during Complex Skill Learning." <em>International Symposium on the Mathematics of Neuroscience</em>, Rhodes, Greece, 2023.</li>
          <li>Jain, R. "Leaps of Insight in Humans Learning a Complex Skill." The Science of Consciousness Conference, April 2024.</li>
          <li>Jain, R. "Evaluating Reinforcement Learning Agents in Hexxed." Pomona College Senior Thesis, advised by Joseph Osborn, May 2024.</li>
          <li>Jain, R., et al. Working paper unifying predictive neural networks and attention-based memory mechanisms, Allen Institute (Mihalas Lab), in preparation.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
